## Introduction

C++ templates are a powerful feature that allows writing generic and reusable code. Template metaprogramming takes this further by performing computations at compile-time.

## Function Templates

Function templates allow you to write a single function that works with different data types.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T max(T a, T b) {
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}
```

## Class Templates

Class templates are used to create generic classes, such as the STL containers.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Box {
private:
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}
    T getValue() { return value; }
};
```

## Variadic Templates

Variadic templates allow templates to accept an arbitrary number of arguments.

```cpp
template<typename... Args>
void print(Args... args) {
    (std::cout << ... << args) << '\n';
}
```

## Conclusion

Templates are essential for modern C++ programming, enabling high-performance, type-safe, and generic code.
